PLACES=[
N('athens-lunatic-asylum-oh','Athens Lunatic Asylum (The Ridges, Athens State Hospital)','Athens','oh','inst','standing',39.32023,-82.1094,'exact','open','walk','','1868–1993',
  "Ohio's asylum for the southeast, built to the Kirkbride plan: a central administration block with long stepped wings for men on one side and women on the other. Levi T. Scofield of Cleveland was the architect and Herman Haerlin of Cincinnati laid out the grounds. Wikipedia and Ohio University date the ground-breaking to November 5, 1868, while SAH Archipedia and KirkbrideBuildings.com say work began in 1867; it opened on January 9, 1874. About 1900 cottages designed by Frank Packard were added, and the hospital grew to a farm of over 1,000 acres; its peak is given as over 2,000 patients by SAH Archipedia and about 1,800 in the 1950s by Ohio University. The last patients moved to a new hospital in 1993 and the state handed the grounds to Ohio University, which named them The Ridges.",
  "Four storeys of red brick (SAH Archipedia: 'built entirely of local bricks') with stone belt courses, round-arched windows with hood moulds, and square towers either side of the entrance. A tall white metal porch, added late in the 1800s and supplied by a bridge maker in Springfield, Ohio, fronts the administration block. The wings' turrets and the roof cupolas are gone. The administration block, now Lin Hall, holds the Kennedy Museum of Art; much of the rest held offices, studios and storage, and KirkbrideBuildings.com reported much of it still empty. A detached tuberculosis ward on the hill, Cottage B, was pulled down by the university in 2013. Three hospital cemeteries lie on the grounds, with some 1,930 burials, most under numbered stones, restored by volunteers since 2000. The farm's cow barn is a separate entry.",
  '','Ohio University, 100 Ridges Circle. The Kennedy Museum of Art in Lin Hall is free; closed on university holidays. Visitor parking is in Lot 200 across from the entrance: four free signed museum spaces, the rest paid by meter or phone (the museum\'s own page). The other buildings are university offices and closed parts: see them from the drives.',
  [W('Athens Lunatic Asylum'),['National Register record: Athens State Hospital (80002936)','https://npgallery.nps.gov/AssetDetail/NRIS/80002936'],['SAH Archipedia: Athens State Hospital','https://sah-archipedia.org/node/79160'],['KirkbrideBuildings.com: Athens','https://www.kirkbridebuildings.com/buildings/athens/index.html'],['Ohio University: The Ridges, history','https://www.ohio.edu/ridges/history'],['Kennedy Museum of Art: plan your visit','https://www.ohio.edu/museum/visit']],
  conf='mixed',brick='yes',bcol='red',hours='Tue, Wed, Fri 10–5; Thu 10–8; Sat–Sun 1–5; closed university holidays',
  arch='Levi T. Scofield',archw="Wikipedia's infobox and SAH Archipedia name Levi T. Scofield as architect; SAH Archipedia credits the later cottages to Frank Packard, built by Henry O'Blenness"),
N('athens-state-hospital-cow-barn-oh','Athens State Hospital Cow Barn (Dairy Barn Arts Center)','Athens','oh','landmark','standing',39.31454,-82.11503,'exact','open','walk','','1914',
  "The Athens asylum bought the Bower farm and its dairy in 1912, and in 1914 built this cow barn beside the old farmhouse for $10,000; the dairy gave patients work and training. Empty for about ten years and set to be pulled down in 1977, it was saved by a citizens' group led by Harriet and Ora Anderson, listed on the National Register in 1978, and opened as an arts centre with its first Quilt National show in 1979. It lies half a mile from the rest of the hospital and was left out of the hospital's historic district.",
  'A long Colonial Revival barn of concrete, partly shingled, under a slate gambrel roof with three ventilator towers on the ridge and a row of dormers; the main door sits in the middle of the long side with six bays of windows either way. Inside are galleries for exhibitions; the wooded Ora Anderson Trail climbs the hillside behind.',
  '','8000 Dairy Lane, run by the Dairy Barn Arts Center (a nonprofit). Galleries $10, children 5 to 12 $5, members and under-5s free; free parking on site (the centre\'s own page).',
  [W('Athens State Hospital Cow Barn'),['Dairy Barn Arts Center: history','https://dairybarn.org/about/history/'],['Dairy Barn Arts Center: visit','https://dairybarn.org/visit-us/']],
  brick='no',hours='Wed–Sun noon–5; closed Mon and Tue',
  apart='athens-lunatic-asylum-oh: the hospital farm\'s barn, half a mile from the main building, with its own Register listing and its own opening hours'),
N('columbus-state-hospital-oh','Columbus State Hospital site (Central Ohio Lunatic Asylum)','Columbus','oh','inst','gone',39.95833,-83.05556,'site','view','drive','','1838–1991',
  "Ohio's first state asylum, the Lunatic Asylum of Ohio, was set up by an act of March 5, 1835 and opened in 1838 on East Broad Street. That building burned in November 1868, killing six patients, and the hospital was rebuilt on this hill west of the Statehouse as an immense Kirkbride building: Ohio State's library history says Governor Hayes dedicated it in 1870, KirkbrideBuildings.com that it took seven years and over $1.5 million and was finished in 1877. It was said to be the largest building in the country until the Pentagon. Planned for about 850, it held almost 3,000 patients by 1935. It closed in the late 1980s, was put on the National Register in 1986 in a failed attempt to save it, and was pulled down: in 1991 by one account, between 1991 and 1996 by Wikipedia's (by S.G. Loewendick & Sons).",
  'Nothing of the hospital building is left: the headquarters of the Ohio Department of Transportation and the Department of Public Safety stand on the grounds. The hospital\'s small cemeteries remain on the Hilltop, under plaques, with many stones marked only by numbers; KirkbrideBuildings.com counts four, WOSU two.',
  '','West Broad Street on the Hilltop, about 1960 to 1980 West Broad. State office grounds: look from the street. Where the cemeteries are, and whether visitors may walk in, was not checked.',
  [W('Columbus State Hospital'),['KirkbrideBuildings.com: Columbus State Hospital','https://mail.kirkbridebuildings.com/buildings/columbus/'],['Ohio State University Libraries: Ohio State Hospital','https://library.osu.edu/site/mhcb/2011/09/26/ohio-state-hospital/'],['WOSU: the history of the Columbus insane cemetery','https://www.wosu.org/news/2020-01-10/curious-cbus-whats-the-history-of-the-columbus-insane-cemetery']],
  conf='mixed',brick='?'),
N('ohio-institution-feeble-minded-youth-oh','Ohio Institution for Feeble-Minded Youth site (Columbus State School)','Columbus','oh','inst','gone',39.952188,-83.044941,'area','view','drive','','1865–1987',
  "Founded by an act of April 17, 1857, the third such state school in the country, it began in a rented mansion and moved to this Hilltop land, bought in 1864; the main building, begun in 1865, took its first residents in July 1868. Wings followed in 1876 and 1878, largely built by the institution's own adult residents. Renamed the Ohio Institution for Feeble-Minded Youth in 1881, it lost its main building to fire on November 18, 1881 and rebuilt within three years; another fire took most of the south wing in 1895. It became the Columbus State School in 1945 and the Columbus Developmental Center in 1980. A caption to an old photograph of the main building says it was pulled down on August 17, 1987.",
  'The large main building, with lancet-arched doors and windows, buttresses, turrets and gables, is gone. The Columbus Developmental Center, a state home for about 100 people, stands on the campus just south of the old site, and the institution\'s cemetery, with about 650 known graves, is on its grounds off Sullivant Avenue.',
  '','1601 West Broad Street. An active state residential centre: look from the public street only.',
  [W('Columbus Developmental Center'),[AP+'Columbus Feeble Minded Institute','https://www.asylumprojects.org/index.php/Columbus_Feeble_Minded_Institute'],['Old photograph: main building of the Institution for Feeble-Minded Youth','https://oldphotogallery.com/places-people/78-main-building-of-the-institution-for-feeble-minded-youth-completed-in-1868-rebuilt-in-1881-and-demolished-on-august-17-1987-circa-1901/']],
  conf='mixed',brick='?'),
N('cleveland-state-hospital-oh','Cleveland State Hospital site (Northern Ohio Lunatic Asylum, Newburgh)','Cleveland','oh','inst','gone',41.44138,-81.62328,'area','view','drive','','1852–1977',
  "Funded by the legislature in 1852 and built on land in Newburgh given by the family of James A. Garfield, the Northern Ohio Lunatic Asylum opened on March 5, 1855 with 100 beds, built by John Gill of Cleveland under a $69,800 contract. Wings added in 1860 and 1861 and more in 1870 made it 900 feet long, but on September 25, 1872 fire destroyed most of it, and a larger Kirkbride building was put up for 640 patients (650 in the Encyclopedia of Cleveland History). Later the Newburgh and then the Cleveland State Hospital, it was exposed by Life magazine's 'Bedlam 1946'. The state began winding it down in 1972 and made it the Cleveland Developmental Center in 1975. The old main building on Turney Road was demolished in 1977, and the remaining buildings later gave way to housing.",
  'Nothing of the asylum is known to stand: the site is a housing development. Pin is the address Cleveland Historical gives, not a mapped footprint.',
  '','Turney Road, Cleveland. Private homes: look from the public street.',
  [['Encyclopedia of Cleveland History: Cleveland State Hospital','https://case.edu/ech/articles/c/cleveland-state-hospital'],['Cleveland Historical: Cleveland State Hospital','https://clevelandhistorical.org/items/show/576'],['us-data.org: Cleveland State Hospital','https://www.us-data.org/oh/cuyahoga/institution/stateasylum/cleveland-st-hosp.html'],[AP+'Cleveland State Hospital','https://www.asylumprojects.org/index.php/Cleveland_State_Hospital']],
  conf='mixed',brick='?',builder='John Gill',archw='us-data.org: John Gill of Cleveland won the building contract for $69,800 (the first building, 1852 to 1855)'),
N('massillon-state-hospital-oh','Massillon State Hospital (Eastern Ohio Insane Asylum)','Massillon','oh','inst','standing',40.76763,-81.50828,'site','view','drive','','1893–2001',
  "Authorized on March 31, 1892, the Eastern Ohio Insane Asylum was begun in 1893 with McKinley Hall and opened on September 6, 1898 with 300 patients from 21 counties. Frank L. Packard's firm won the work with a cottage plan, one of the first state hospitals laid out that way, and designed about eighteen buildings: cottages, dining, kitchen and bakery, laundry, power and boiler houses, hospital and infirmary, and the superintendent's and steward's houses. It held 3,100 patients in 1950. Patients were moved out from the mid-1970s, and the state hospital, renamed several times, now runs as Heartland Behavioral Healthcare.",
  'An active state psychiatric hospital on the old grounds. The Packard history pages say only two of the original buildings remain, without naming them; which ones, and what they look like, was not checked.',
  '','3000 Erie Street South. A working state hospital: look from the public road only.',
  [[AP+'Massillon State Hospital','https://www.asylumprojects.org/index.php/Massillon_State_Hospital'],['Frank Packard pages (ghmchs.org): Massillon State Hospital','https://ghmchs.org/massillon-state-hospital'],['Frank Packard pages: Massillon State Hospital for the insane','https://ghmchs2.org/PackardFiles/MassillonStHosp.html']],
  conf='mixed',brick='?',arch='Frank L. Packard',archw="ghmchs.org: Packard's firm was chosen for its cottage-plan proposal and designed the buildings"),
N('lima-state-hospital-oh','Lima State Hospital for the Criminally Insane','Lima','oh','inst','standing',40.78256,-84.10061,'exact','view','drive','','1908–2004',
  "The state's hospital for the criminally insane, authorized in April 1906 for those found insane in prison or the courts and for patients too dangerous for the other asylums. Frank Packard of Columbus designed it, with Ralph Snyder as associate; the Olmsted Brothers apparently laid out the grounds; and the National Concrete Fireproofing Company of Cleveland built it from August 1908 to January 1915, for more than $2.2 million in the Register document ($2.1 million in Asylum Projects). It opened on July 19, 1915. Built of poured reinforced concrete, walls, floors and stairs alike, it is said to have been the largest poured-concrete building under one roof before the Pentagon. It held over 1,400 patients in 1958. From 1982 to 1984 the state turned it into the Lima Correctional Institution, which closed in June 2004.",
  'A huge Colonial Revival building on a pavilion plan: one corridor runs in an oval round a courtyard, with ward wings off the long sides and administration and service wings at the ends, all of reinforced concrete faced with red brick. The 2021 Register document called the main building closed and vacant, inside its own security fence; the Oakwood Correctional Facility uses the old 1950s psychopathic unit to the northwest, and the Allen-Oakwood prison stands to the south. A red brick nurses\' residence, staff houses, a powerhouse and a machine shop survive nearby.',
  '','North West Street, north of Lima; the old hospital building stands about 650 m east of the road, beside the fenced Oakwood compound. State prison land, fenced and guarded: look only from the public roads; how much of the building shows from them was not checked.',
  [[AP+'Lima State Hospital','https://www.asylumprojects.org/index.php/Lima_State_Hospital'],MPD,W('Allen-Oakwood Correctional Institution')],
  conf='mixed',brick='yes',bcol='red',arch='Frank L. Packard; Ralph Snyder',builder='National Concrete Fireproofing Company',
  archw='National Register cover document (2006): the commission retained Frank Packard of Columbus as architect, Ralph Snyder associate architect; the contract went to the National Concrete Fireproofing Company of Cleveland'),
N('orient-state-institute-oh','Orient State Institute (Pickaway Correctional Institution)','Orient','oh','inst','standing',39.79673,-83.15643,'area','view','drive','','1898–1983',
  "Begun in 1898 as a farm colony of the Columbus institution for the feeble-minded and opened in 1902, laid out on the cottage plan; it became a separate institution on July 15, 1926, and in 1950 came back under the Columbus State School. In 1983 the state passed the Orient Developmental Center to the prison department, which made it a training academy, the Orient Correctional Institution (closed in 2002) and a pre-release centre that became the Pickaway Correctional Institution in 1984.",
  "The Register's 2021 cover document says the prison's grounds still hold several early twentieth-century buildings of the Orient State Institute; which ones, and their condition, was not checked. The rest is a working prison.",
  '','11781 State Route 762, Orient. An active state prison: look from the public road only.',
  [[AP+'Orient Institute','https://asylumprojects.org/index.php/Orient_Institute'],W('Pickaway Correctional Institution'),MPD],
  conf='mixed',brick='?'),
N('ohio-hospital-for-epileptics-water-towers-oh','Ohio Hospital for Epileptics stone water towers','Gallipolis','oh','inst','standing',38.8214,-82.1868,'area','view','drive','','1890–1976',
  "Ohio set up a hospital for people with epilepsy here in 1890, on ground that had been a Union hospital in the Civil War; the marker calls it the first of its kind in the country. It opened on November 30, 1893 on the cottage plan: by 1901 an administration building, thirteen cottages, a school, shops, dining rooms and a power plant, for up to 1,060 patients. It closed in 1976 and its buildings are gone. Its sandstone water towers survive: the marker says three, built in 1892 by local tradesmen, Asylum Projects and the Packard pages say two, built by Ohio Penitentiary inmates, and the Register record gives 1895. They supplied the hospital until 1950, were restored in 1981 and 1982, and have been on the National Register since 1978.",
  'Round towers of uncoursed sandstone quarried in the hills nearby. An Ohio Historical Marker (3-27) stands on Mill Creek Road just south of Ohio Avenue, across from the Haskins Park shelter.',
  '','Mill Creek Road, Gallipolis. Seen from the road by the marker; who owns the ground the towers stand on was not checked.',
  [['National Register record: Ohio Hospital for Epileptics Stone Water Towers (78002069)','https://npgallery.nps.gov/AssetDetail/9b6a9b0c-2b96-4203-bba7-d2257e988404'],['HMdb: Stone Water Towers marker','https://www.hmdb.org/m.asp?m=122906'],[AP+'Ohio Hospital for Epileptics','https://asylumprojects.org/index.php/Ohio_Hospital_for_Epileptics'],['Frank Packard pages (ghmchs.org): Gallipolis Epileptic Hospital','https://www.ghmchs.org/gallipolis-epileptic-hospital'],['TrailLink: the towers, Gallia County Hike and Bike Trail','https://www.traillink.com/trail-photo/gallia-county-hike--bike-trail_195364']],
  conf='mixed',brick='no',arch='John Andrew Stirling',archw='National Register record 78002069 names Stirling, John Andrew as architect of the towers'),
N('toledo-state-hospital-new-cemetery-oh','Toledo State Hospital New Cemetery','Toledo','oh','burial','standing',41.6171,-83.61353,'site','view','walk','','1922–1973',
  "The second burial ground of the Toledo State Hospital, opened in 1922 when the old cemetery filled; burials went on until 1973. More than 1,000 people lie here under small numbered concrete markers keyed to the hospital's burial log. The hospital itself opened in 1888 to Edward O. Fallis's cottage plan, 34 buildings on 150 acres, and was razed in the early 1990s; Northwest Ohio Psychiatric Hospital is on its land. A volunteer committee has been raising and restoring the sunken markers of both cemeteries; by 2011 it had restored between 1,200 and 1,300 of the 1,994 it counts.",
  'Rows of numbered markers, and an Ohio Historical Marker put up in 2010 by the University of Toledo and the Ohio Historical Society, by East Campus Drive.',
  '','On the University of Toledo\'s Health Science Campus, on East Campus Drive about 0.3 mile south of Arlington Avenue, across from a school parking lot entrance (HMdb). Whether visitors may walk among the graves was not checked.',
  [['HMdb: The Toledo State Hospital New Cemetery, 1922-1973','https://www.hmdb.org/m.asp?m=30782'],['UToledo News: cemeteries restored, 2011','https://news.utoledo.edu/?p=12609'],[AP+'Toledo State Hospital','https://www.asylumprojects.org/index.php/Toledo_Insane_Asylum'],['University of Toledo Libraries: aerial of the former hospital grounds','https://www.utoledo.edu/library/virtualexhibitions/dvx/large/ex1/ex1_TSH_aerial.html']],
  brick='?'),
]
